    "events": [
      {
        "met_days": 0,
        "type": "launch",
        "label": "Launch — Saturn V",
        "description": "Kennedy Space Center LC-39A, 1970-04-11 19:13 UTC. Apollo 13 — Lovell + Swigert + Haise — bound for the Fra Mauro highlands. The launch had slipped from March to 11 April 1970 to allow more planning time; Swigert replaced Ken Mattingly (suspected German measles exposure) two days before launch."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 0.117,
        "type": "tli_or_tmi",
        "label": "Trans-lunar injection",
        "description": "S-IVB third-stage burn at 2h 35m, sending Odyssey + Aquarius on a 3-day translunar coast.",
        "dv_km_s": 3.05
      },
      {
        "met_days": 1.2,
        "type": "tcm",
        "label": "MCC-2 — first midcourse correction",
        "description": "Mid-cruise trajectory tweak. Planned + executed before the anomaly."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 2.33,
        "type": "anomaly",
        "label": "Oxygen tank 2 explosion — \"Houston, we've had a problem\"",
        "description": "1970-04-14 03:08 UTC. Oxygen tank 2 in the Service Module ruptured during a routine cryo stir, venting both O₂ tanks and crippling the CSM's fuel-cell electrical power. Cause was later traced to overheated heater thermostat switches (rated 28 V, fed 65 V during pre-launch ground detanking) that damaged the Teflon insulation of the tank's fan wiring; in flight a short in those wires ignited it. Mission objective immediately switched from \"land on the Moon\" to \"get the crew home alive\"."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 2.56,
        "type": "tcm",
        "label": "Free-return burn",
        "description": "1970-04-14. With the SPS engine ruled out (could compound the SM damage), the LM descent engine fired for 34 seconds to put Aquarius + Odyssey on a faster free-return trajectory back to Earth — using the Moon's gravity instead of a burn."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 3.21,
        "type": "flyby",
        "label": "Moon — free-return flyby",
        "description": "1970-04-15 00:21 UTC, closest approach 254 km above the far side. Apollo 13 reached 400,171 km from Earth there — the farthest humans had travelled from Earth until Artemis II (April 2026). No lunar landing — Aquarius (the LM, designed for 45 hours) had to keep three astronauts alive for 87 hours instead."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 3.31,
        "type": "tcm",
        "label": "PC+2 — second LM descent-engine burn",
        "description": "Another LM descent-engine burn refines the Earth-return trajectory + targets the splashdown ellipse."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 5.95,
        "type": "earth_return",
        "label": "Splashdown — Pacific Ocean",
        "description": "1970-04-17 18:07 UTC. Apollo 13 splashed down 6.5 km from USS Iwo Jima, near Samoa. The mission was reclassified a \"successful failure\" — no Moon landing, but the crew survived and the systems-engineering lessons fed directly into Apollo 14–17 + later programs. Mattingly later flew Apollo 16."
      }
    ],
